ing_rate=0.001),\n    loss=\"binary_crossentropy\",\n    metrics=[\n    \"accuracy\",\n    tf.keras.metrics.AUC(name=\"auc\")\n]\n)\n","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-08-04T15:07:50.682306Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-08-04T15:07:50.682638Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-08-04T15:07:50.702032Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-08-04T15:07:50.682617Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-08-04T15:07:50.701544Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":49},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Model Compilation\n\nThe BiLSTM model was compiled using the **Adam optimizer**, **Binary Cross-Entropy loss function**, and **Accuracy** as the evaluation metric. These choices were made based on the nature of the dataset and the binary classification problem.\n\n### 1. Optimizer: Adam\n\nThe Adam (Adaptive Moment Estimation) optimizer was selected because it combines the advantages of Momentum and RMSProp. It automatically adapts the learning rate for each parameter, resulting in faster convergence and more stable training. Adam is widely used in Natural Language Processing tasks due to its efficiency and robustness.\n\n### 2. Learning Rate: 0.001\n\nA learning rate of **0.001** was chosen because it is the default and most commonly recommended value for the Adam optimizer. It provides a good balance between learning speed and training stability. Higher learning rates may cause unstable training, while lower values increase the training time without significant performance improvements for this dataset.\n\n### 3. Loss Function: Binary Cross-Entropy\n\nThe problem was formulated as a **binary classification** task, where each question-option pair is labelled as:\n\n- **1** → Correct answer\n- **0** → Incorrect answer\n\nSince the output layer consists of a single neuron with a **Sigmoid activation function**, Binary Cross-Entropy is the most appropriate loss function for measuring the prediction error.\n\n### 4. Evaluation Metrics: Accuracy and AUC\n\nTwo evaluation metrics were used during training:\n\n- **Accuracy** measures the percentage of correctly classified question-option pairs and provides an intuitive indication of the model's performance.\n- **AUC (Area Under the ROC Curve)** evaluates the model's ability to distinguish between the positive (correct answer) and negative (incorrect answer) classes across different classification thresholds. Unlike accuracy, AUC is less sensitive to the chosen decision threshold and provides a more comprehensive measure of classification performance.\n\nUsing both metrics allows the model to be evaluated from different perspectives, providing a more reliable assessment of its learning behaviour.\n\n### Summary\n\n| Hyperparameter | Selected Value | Justification |\n|----------------|---------------|---------------|\n| Optimizer | Adam | Fast convergence and adaptive learning rates for NLP tasks |\n| Learning Rate | 0.001 | Stable default value for Adam that balances speed and convergence |\n| Loss Function | Binary Cross-Entropy | Suitable for binary classification using Sigmoid output |\n| Evaluation Metric | Accuracy, AUC| Accuracy measures correct predictions, while AUC evaluates the model's discrimination ability across thresholds |","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Model Training\n\nThe model was trained using **EarlyStopping** and **ModelCheckpoint** callbacks to improve generalization and avoid unnecessary training.\n\n### 1. EarlyStopping\n\nThe EarlyStopping callback monitors the validation loss during training. If the validation loss does not improve for several consecutive epochs, the training process is stopped automatically. This helps prevent overfitting and reduces unnecessary computation.\n\nThe best model weights are restored automatically after training.\n\n### 2. ModelCheckpoint\n\nThe ModelCheckpoint callback saves the model whenever the validation loss improves. This ensures that the best-performing model is preserved, even if subsequent epochs lead to overfitting.\n\n### Training Configuration\n\n- Optimizer: Adam\n- Loss Function: Binary Cross-Entropy\n- Metrics: Accuracy and AUC\n- Batch Size: 32\n- Maximum Epochs: 10\n- EarlyStopping Patience: 2 epochs","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Convert text to NumPy string arrays\nX_train_tf = X_train.astype(str).to_numpy()\nX_val_tf = X_val.astype(str).to_numpy()\n\n# Convert labels to NumPy integer arrays\ny_train_tf = y_train.to_numpy(dtype=np.int32)\ny_val_tf = y_val.to_numpy(dtype=np.int32)\n","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-08-04T15:07:50.702811Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-08-04T15:07:50.703086Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-08-04T15:07:50.708505Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-08-04T15:07:50.703052Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-08-04T15:07:50.707715Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":50},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"TensorFlow models were trained using NumPy arrays rather than Pandas Series to ensure compatibility with the Keras training pipeline. The text inputs were explicitly converted to string arrays and the labels to integer arrays, preventing datatype-related errors during training.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# ==========================================================\n# Train BiLSTM Model\n# ==========================================================\n\n\n\nearly_stop = EarlyStopping(\n    monitor=\"val_loss\",\n    patience=2,\n    restore_best_weights=True\n)\n\ncheckpoint = ModelCheckpoint(\n    \"best_bilstm.keras\",\n    monitor=\"val_loss\",\n    save_best_only=True,\n    verbose=1\n)\n\nhistory = model.fit(\n    X_train_tf,\n    y_train_tf,\n    validation_data=(X_val_tf, y_val_tf),\n    epochs=10,\n    batch_size=32,\n    callbacks=[early_stop, checkpoint],\n    verbose=1\n)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-08-04T15:07:50.709635Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-08-04T15:07:50.709931Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-08-04T15:08:30.56